## Problem

Shoppers describe what they want in sentences, for example "waterproof women's hiking boots under $150, size 8, 4 stars and up". Keyword search handles that badly, and adding AI search that guesses wrong is worse than not having it.

## Approach

- **One filter schema.** A single Zod schema defines every filter the store supports. The model's output has to match it, or it's rejected.
- **Editable chips.** What the search understood is shown as chips that you can remove or edit, so you can correct it without retyping.
- **Honest about gaps.** Words that couldn't be mapped to a filter are shown, so it's clear why the results look the way they do.
- **Normal search still works.** Facets, sorting, pagination, and shareable URLs, like a real storefront.
- **Two providers and an offline mode.** Claude and OpenAI sit behind one small interface, and an offline parser keeps the demo working without API keys.
- **Evals.** An evaluation suite runs from the command line and scores the parser per provider.

## Stack

Next.js, TypeScript, Zod, the Claude and OpenAI APIs, and Tailwind CSS.

## Outcome

A public, runnable version of the natural-language filter pattern I've shipped in production, described in the [marketplace search project](/work/marketplace-search). You can try the demo, read the code, and run the evals yourself.
